{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U6"],
      "pins": ["U6.16", "U6.15"],
      "nets": ["STEP_NUT_A", "STEP (U6 pin 16)"],
      "observed": "On the PERISTALTIC PUMP DRIVERS sheet, the Nutrient-A driver U6 (TMC2209) has its STEP pin (16) marked with a no-connect 'X', while the green STEP_NUT_A signal wire is attached to pin 15 (DIR). The two sibling drivers U5 (Ph↓) and U7 (Nutrient-B) are wired the opposite way: their STEP_NUT/STEP_PH_DN green wires enter pin 16 (STEP) and pin 15 (DIR) carries the no-connect 'X'.",
      "expected": "The specification states each TMC2209 'drives a 2-phase coil output ... plus individual STEP lines', i.e. the per-pump step signal must reach the STEP pin (16). The DIR pin (15) is the direction input and, for a unidirectional peristaltic pump, is left unconnected (as correctly done on U5 and U7).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 'PERISTALTIC PUMP DRIVERS', 'Nutrition Pump A' block U6: red no-connect marker on pin 16 'STEP', green wire 'STEP_NUT_A' on pin 15 'DIR' (compare U5/U7 where green STEP line is on pin 16 and 'X' is on pin 15)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "each drives a 2-phase coil output (/A_xx+/-, /B_xx+/-) to connectors H7/H8/H9, with its own current-sense resistor pair (R30–R35, 110 mΩ) and a shared single-wire UART (STEP_BUS) plus individual STEP lines"}
      ],
      "impact": "The Nutrient-A peristaltic pump will not step: its STEP input floats (undefined) and the step pulses are instead applied to the DIR pin, so the driver never produces the coil stepping sequence. Nutrient-A dosing fails (and the floating STEP pin may cause erratic/undefined coil states).",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The power train, isolated pH/EC I2C bridges, float interlocks and the U5/U7 stepper drivers match the specification, but the Nutrient-A stepper driver U6 has its STEP and DIR pins swapped (STEP pin 16 left no-connect, STEP_NUT_A routed to DIR pin 15), which would stop the Nutrient-A dosing pump from stepping."
}

